  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MDG4/5 Africa H4+</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">MDG4/5 Africa H4+</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">St&#xf6;d till H4+ (UNICEF, UNFPA, WHO, UNAIDS, UN Women och V&#xe4;rldsbanken) f&#xf6;r att uppn&#xe5; MDG 4 och 5 p&#xe5; landniv&#xe5; i sex l&#xe4;nder: Elfenbenskusten,  Etiopien, Guinea-Bissau, Kamerun, Liberia och Zimbabwe. Avtalspart &#xe4;r UNFPA som utsedd administrativ agent f&#xf6;r H4+-samarbetet. St&#xf6;det g&#xe5;r   f&#xf6;retr&#xe4;desvis till att implementera l&#xe4;ndernas nationella planer f&#xf6;r   m&#xf6;dra- och barnh&#xe4;lsa f&#xf6;r perioden 2012-2015.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Support to H4+ to achieve MDG 4 and 5 on country level in six         countries (above). Support is targeting service delivery in           accordance with the national plans for maternal, newborn and child    health for the period 2012-2015.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The objective of the H6 Joint Programme is to provide catalytic and strategic support to national health systems to address the root causes of poor maternal and child health outcomes in 10 countries: Burkina Faso, Cameroon, C&#xf4;te d&#x2019;Ivoire, Democratic Republic of the Congo, Ethiopia, Guinea-Bissau, Liberia, Sierra Leone, Zambia and Zimbabwe, along with global-level activities.
The programme implemented by the H6 Partnership (UNAIDS, UNFPA, UNICEF, UN Women, WHO and World Bank), has received support from Canada and the Swedish International Development Cooperation Agency (Sida) for a total of $99.76 million during the period 2012 - 2018. Collaboration with Canada started in 2012 and ended in 2016, whereas collaboration with Sida covers 2013 to 2018. Therefore, this report reviews progress for 2017 for Sida support only.</narrative>
  </description>
